t is an axis like this one."],"caption":["This is the plane $z = 0$ about one world line of the dust in Gödel's universe, at one moment of the cylindrical chart's $t$, drawn as a surface in flat space so that every distance along it is the distance the metric gives. On it the circle $r$ has circumference $2\\pi\\sqrt{2}\\,\\sinh r\\sqrt{1 - \\sinh^2 r}/\\omega$, which grows out to $\\sinh^2 r = 1/2$ and then shrinks, so the surface curls back toward the axis, and at $\\sinh^2 r = 1/\\sqrt{2}$ the circles shrink faster than the distance out to them and the drawing stops.","At $\\sinh r = 1$ the circles are null, and beyond it they are closed timelike curves, which Kurt Gödel found in 1949. The universe is homogeneous, so every world line of the dust is an axis like this one, and it has no moment of time that is space everywhere: this surface is a moment only near its axis."]}]}
